ned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317256 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a3280171-70dd-50e2-8570-67e489365512" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186317256 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cfd6da222fb36d06be1153ac0e398e567f8b9fb8cd1cd9e21f1bb35ed131f8d.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}(x+1)=-2+1=-1\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(-2)=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/a5/a59fad69c9790a2b899c21fd730f779c28bc9b70f44a67851ad5ca92bff21951.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425552 #edb/ref 1080 "True" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0cf9be56-efa9-5355-8976-ae2a23fa9c57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425560 #edb/ref 1080 "True" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425560 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425560 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "19361797-c18f-5c7